# Grading AI-Supported Work

Once expectations are clear, the next step is designing and grading work in ways that reflect those expectations.

In an AI-supported classroom, strong assessment design is the most effective way to support learning and reduce misuse. Rather than focusing only on what students produce, the goal is to understand how they think, make decisions, and improve their work.

<div style="max-width:900px; margin:20px auto; font-family:ui-sans-serif, system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, Helvetica, Arial; color:#292F36;">

<div style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:16px;">

<!-- Card 1 -->
<div style="border:2px solid #8C52BA; border-radius:18px; padding:18px; background:#FFFFFF; box-shadow:0 4px 12px rgba(41,47,54,0.06);">
<div style="font-size:20px; font-weight:800; margin-bottom:10px; color:#1F2933;">
Design for Visible Thinking
</div>
<ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">
<li><strong>Emphasize process over product</strong> — Use drafts, revisions, annotations, or checkpoints to show how ideas develop over time</li>
<li><strong>Incorporate explanation and reflection</strong> — Have students explain their reasoning and reflect on their approach and learning</li>
<li><strong>Use in-class components and check-ins</strong> — Writing, discussion, and conferences help reveal thinking in real time</li>
<li><strong>Incorporate personalization</strong> — Connect work to students’ experiences and perspectives to encourage authentic responses</li>
</ul>
</div>

<!-- Card 2 -->
<div style="border:2px solid #8C52BA; border-radius:18px; padding:18px; background:#FFFFFF; box-shadow:0 4px 12px rgba(41,47,54,0.06);">
<div style="font-size:20px; font-weight:800; margin-bottom:10px; color:#1F2933;">
Grade What Matters Most
</div>
<ul style="margin:0; padding-left:18px; font-size:15px; line-height:1.6;">
<li><strong>Student thinking</strong> — Prioritize clarity of ideas, reasoning, and demonstrated understanding</li>
<li><strong>Revision and improvement</strong> — Focus on how students use feedback to refine their work</li>
<li><strong>Critical use of AI</strong> — Evaluate how well students analyze outputs and make thoughtful choices</li>
<li><strong>Overall approach</strong> — Emphasize thinking, effort, and decision-making over a perfect final product</li>
</ul>
</div>

</div>
</div>

## Focus on Design Over Detection

When assessments are designed to make thinking visible, it becomes less necessary—and less effective—to rely on AI detection tools.

AI detection tools are often unreliable and can incorrectly flag student work, especially for multilingual learners or students using structured writing. Over-reliance on these tools can undermine trust without meaningfully supporting learning.

Instead, focus on creating opportunities for students to demonstrate their understanding through their process, reasoning, and decisions.
